16 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Comparison table of antibodies with and without Fc fragment. With Fc fragment: large size, long half-life, Fc-mediated effector function, limited penetration, potential immunogenicity, systemic toxicity, well-established production. Preferred for prolonged therapy and systemic action. Without Fc fragment: compact size, short half-life, rapid binding, good penetration, no ADCC/CDC, low toxicity, but unstable production. Preferred for rapid response and subcutaneous administration.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (Diagram depicting the key mechanisms of action of bispecific antibodies. It is divided into four sections: Immune Cell Targeting, Immune Checkpoint Blockade, Tumor Signaling Pathway Inhibition, and Soluble Cytokine Targeting. Each section contains specific examples and descriptions of mechanisms, such as MHC-independent cytotoxicity, T-cell \215unlocking,\216 dual receptor inhibition, and dual cytokine neutralization. The diagram includes visuals of antibodies and cells like T cells and tumor cells, illustrating interactions and therapeutic targets.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (Diagram detailing the adverse effects of bispecific immunotherapy and mitigation strategies. It shows four categories: \215On-target, off-tumor toxicity\216 with strategies like step-up dosing; \215Physicochemical Barriers to Tumor Penetration\216 with strategies like ECM modulation; \215Antigenic Drift\216 with multi-targeting strategies; and \215Immunogenicity & Anti-Drug Antibodies\216 with strategies like humanization of antibody regions. Each category outlines specific effects and corresponding mitigation strategies. An illustration of a vial labeled \215Immunotherapy with Bispecific Constructs\216 connects the categories.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Combination strategies for bispecific constructs in immunotherapy are outlined. Combinations with cellular therapy focus on preventing antigen-negative relapse and enhancing response duration, with limitations of CRS and neurotoxicity. Checkpoint inhibitors prevent T-cell exhaustion but may cause autoimmune complications. Oncolytic viruses convert cold tumors to hot, enhancing T-cell infiltration, with limitations in antiviral immune response.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating strategies for developing multispecific immunotherapy technologies. A rocket icon represents the central concept. Three arrows point to different strategies: Molecular engineering with affinity tuning and multi-specific format; Pro-Drug with examples like proteases, collagenases, and pH; Delivery systems targeting liposomal nanoparticles \(LNP\) using bispecific antibodies \(BsAb\) and producing BsAb in LNP. Icons accompany each strategy for visual emphasis.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 51 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
